The existence of a vector $\mathbf{v}$ such that $\{\mathbf{v}, T(\mathbf{v}), \ldots, T^{n-1}(\mathbf{v})\}$ is linearly independent implies that $T$ has a cyclic vector. This means the minimal polynomial of $T$ equals its characteristic polynomial. When the minimal polynomial equals the characteristic polynomial, each eigenvalue of $T$ corresponds to a single Jordan block (since repeated eigenvalues would require multiple Jordan blocks, which would reduce the minimal polynomial's degree). This ensures that the geometric multiplicity of each eigenvalue is 1, meaning each eigenvalue has a unique eigenvector (up to scalar multiplication). 

